galog"}, "word": "mangmang"}, {"language": {"code": "tr", "name": "Turkish"}, "word": "cahil"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "не́уцький"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "неосві́чений"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "неодуко́ваний"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "неписьме́нний"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "негра́мотний"}, {"language": {"code": "uk", "name": "Ukrainian"}, "word": "те́мний"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "ngu dốt"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "dốt nát"}, {"language": {"code": "vi", "name": "Vietnamese"}, "word": "mù chữ"}, {"language": {"code": "cy", "name": "Welsh"}, "word": "anwybodus"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Not knowing (a fact or facts), unaware (of something).", "tags": [], "examples": ["1851, Walt Whitman, “Art and Artists” in Emory Holloway (editor), The Uncollected Poetry and Prose of Walt Whitman, Garden City, NY: Doubleday, Page & Co., 1921, Volume 1, p. 242,\n[…] perhaps it is sometimes the case that the greatest artists live and die, the world and themselves alike ignorant what they possess."], "quotes": [{"text": "For we would not, brethren, have you ignorant of our trouble which came to us in Asia, that we were pressed out of measure, above strength, insomuch that we despaired even of life:", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, 2 Corinthians 1:8:"}, {"text": "Eve. Somewhat forbids me, which I cannot name;\nFor ignorant of guilt, I fear not shame:\nBut some restraining thought, I know not why,\nTells me, you long should beg, I long deny.", "reference": "1677, John Dryden, The State of Innocence and Fall of Man, London: Henry Herringman, act II, page 14:"}, {"text": "That night he slept the sleep of happiness, blissfully ignorant that he had placed the letters in the wrong envelopes.", "reference": "1921, John T. McCutcheon, The Restless Age, Indianapolis: Bobbs-Merrill, page 179:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "eo", "name": "Esperanto"}, "word": "senscia"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) Ill-mannered, crude.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": ["His manner was at best off-hand, at worst totally ignorant."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Unknown; undiscovered", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": ["1845, Robert Browning, letter addressed to Elizabeth Barrett Browning, cited in Percy Lubbock, Elizabeth Barrett Browning in Her Letters, London: Smith, Elder & Co., 1906, Chapter 4, p. 106,\n[…] as to you, your goodness and understanding will always see to the bottom of involuntary or ignorant faults—always help me to correct them."], "quotes": [{"text": "Alas, what ignorant sin have I committed?", "reference": "c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Othello, the Moore of Venice”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "[…] I beseech you,\nIf you know aught which does behove my knowledge\nThereof to be inform’d, imprison't not\nIn ignorant concealment.", "reference": "c. 1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Winters Tale”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene ii]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Resulting from ignorance; foolish; silly.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[…] his shipping—\nPoor ignorant baubles!— upon our terrible seas,\nLike eggshells moved upon their surges, crack’d\nAs easily ’gainst our rocks:", "reference": "1611 April (first recorded performance), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Cymbeline”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene i]:"}, {"text": "He had never felt a pain or a sorrow, and did not know what they were, in any really informing way. He had no knowledge of them except theoretically—that is to say, intellectually. And of course that is no good. One can never get any but a loose and ignorant notion of such things except by experience.", "reference": "1916, Mark Twain, chapter 8, in Albert Paine, editor, The Mysterious Stranger, New York: Harper & Bros., page 112:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["benighted", "cimmerian", "clueless", "foolish", "ignorant", "ill-informed", "illiterate", "innocent", "lack-learning", "lay", "lewd", "loreless", "nescient", "oblivious", "philistine", "tenebrose", "unaware", "uncultivated", "uncultured", "uneducated", "unenlightened", "unerudite", "uninformed", "unknowing", "unknowledgeable", "unlearned", "unlearnt", "unlettered", "unmindful", "unread", "unscholarly", "unschooled", "untaught", "untrained", "untutored", "unwitting"], "antonyms": ["educated", "familiar", "knowledgable", "learned", "trained", "wise"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɪɡ.nə.ɹənt/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɪɡ.nənt/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "ignorants", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "ignoraunt", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}, {"word": "ignant", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "One who is ignorant.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ignorant", "license": {"na